# Data classification & data security

Resume: `@data-class$`. Status: **approved Oct 8 2026**. Phase 1 (register + Master Admin tab + exposure report) and FloqAi enforcement (phase 5 pulled forward) shipped in **s3.1.21**. Phases 2–4 open; phase 3 needs an explicit owner go-ahead before writing production data. The proposal text below is kept for history; the "Shipped (s3.1.21)" section wins where they differ.

## Shipped (s3.1.21)

### Register

- `functions/data-classification-core.js` (UMD) is the packaged catalog and access model; `floqr-data-classification.js` is a byte-identical copy served to the browser (`scripts/sync-data-classification-client.js`; a test enforces equality).
- Every collection in `firestore.rules` has a catalog row, including nested `suprstrSessions/callerCandidates` and `/calleeCandidates` (test fails otherwise). Row datapoints: `isPublicAccessible`, the five tiers, `ownRecordReadable`, `containsPII`, `retentionDays`, `isSystemAccessible` (always 1), plus derived `classificationLevel` (public / internal / clubConfidential / restricted / secret) and `scope` (platform | club).
- Tiers are cumulative upward; own-record implies Master Admin. Saved overrides live in `dataClassification/{collection with / → __}` — Master Admin read, writes only via SOS2FA callables `seedDataClassification` / `setDataClassification` / `logDataClassificationExport` on the Features & Services audit chain (`dataClass.seeded|changed|exported`).
- **Field rows** (`FIELD_CATALOG`, release-only, shown read-only in the tab): `users.marketingMediaConsent` = restricted, own-record readable, Club Admin of the affiliated club (club scope); `users.email` / `users.phone` ownMaster; `clubEmployeeDesignations.workerEmail` / `workerPhone` ownClubAdmin. Consent collections: `staffMarketingConsentLogs` ownClubAdmin + PII, `staffMarketingConsentThrottle` secret; rules = Master Admin read / server write, throttles closed.
- Classification is **not yet** enforced by Firestore rules (phase 4). The tab’s exposure report (parses the served `./firestore.rules`) lists where rules are broader than the register — 70+ rows at ship time (`users`, `inboxNotifications`, `clubEmployeeDesignations`, `messages`, `roleRequests` reads; signed-in writes on `events`, `inboxNotifications`, …).

### System tier (platform server identity)

`isSystemAccessible` is always 1 and cannot be edited (`validateChange` forces it; not in `FLAGS`). Controls:

1. **Server-only.** `system` is in `ROLES` but not `CLIENT_ROLES`; `resolveViewerRole` never returns it; `access(row, {role: "system"})` is "no" unless the caller passes `server: true` (only server code does). No custom claim or user field grants it.
2. **Least privilege per job.** `SYSTEM_JOBS` lists each job’s purpose and allowlisted collections; `systemJob(job, collection)` throws otherwise. `getFloqAiAccess` reads only through `systemCollection()` (job `floqAiAccess`).
3. **Audited purpose.** Each FloqAi access check writes a structured Cloud Logging line (`event: floqai.access`, job, purpose, role, counts, App Check verdict). Restricted / secret / unclassified denials also write an unchained `floqai.content_denied` row to `featureServiceAuditLogs` (throttled 20 / 10 min per uid or hashed IP in `floqAiAccessThrottle`).
4. **App Check / auth on the fronting callable.** Verdict recorded (`appCheck: verified|missing`); enforcement stays off until hubs send tokens (see App Check note). Auth optional — signed-out callers resolve to `anonymous`.
5. **Never returns more than the caller’s tier.** The callable returns only allowed/denied ids, the caller’s role name, and a club count — never levels, club ids, or other people’s data.
- Honest gaps: other existing Functions still use the Admin SDK directly and have not moved onto `systemJob`; help copy still ships in the public JS bundle, so classification controls what FloqAi **shows**, not secrecy of the text.

### FloqAi enforcement

- Every FloqAi source has an id: `help:<repository id>` or `intent:<curated id>` (`venue-search` is `intent:venue-search`, public). Repository entries carry `audiences`; curated intents are classified in `INTENT_AUDIENCES` in `intent-search.js`. Audience → datapoint: public → isPublicAccessible, patron, serviceMember → regular, privilegedEmployee, venueAdmin → clubAdmin, masterAdmin.
- **Fail closed:** an intent with no `INTENT_AUDIENCES` entry, a malformed id, or an id missing from the server manifest is never shown. Runtime-only DOM popouts (`popout-*`) are not in the manifest, so the server denies them; durable copy must be seeded in the repository.
- **Translations inherit:** localized help keeps the source entry id; the check always runs on the source id (test with a stub localizer).
- **Server:** `functions/floqai-content-classes.json` (built by `scripts/build-floqai-content-classes.js`, drift test) + `getFloqAiAccess({sourceIds ≤ 60})` → keeps the `featureServices/floqAi` guard (`assertFeatureAccess`), resolves role from server facts (Master Admin via `viewerFor`; Club Admin via `clubLocations.adminUids|masterAdminUids|adminEmails` + active `clubAdminAssignments`; staff via non-rejected `clubEmployeeDesignations`, privileged when `rolePermissions` non-empty), ignores anything role-like in the request.
- **Client:** `floqai-access.js` (`FLOQRFloqAiAccess`) batches ids, caches verdicts per viewer (reset on auth change), and `intent-search.js` renders only server-allowed ids on `floqai.html` (“Checking what your account can see…” while pending). If the callable is unreachable it falls back to the packaged local classification (still fail closed); a feature denial hides everything. `index.html` keeps the local path only.
- Club Admin “scoped to their club”: FloqAi content is platform-scoped help today; club-scoped data (venue records) stays behind rules / venue callables.

### Master Admin → Security → Data classification

Grid (Public, five tiers, read-only System column, Own, PII, retention, level, rules read/write breadth, per-row Save with reason), sensitive-fields table, simulator (all roles incl. System), exposure report, Export CSV (logged). Panel is SOS2FA-gated (`ENTITY_MGMT_PANELS`). Chrome keys `dataClass.*` + `master.securityDataClassification` in all 11 packs; help `help-data-classification` (masterAdmin, English).

### Still open

- Phase 2: custom claims (`role`, `clubIds`) trigger; rules accept claim or legacy check.
- Phase 3: PII split (e.g. `users` → `userPrivate`) — dry-run script only; owner go-ahead required before production writes.
- Phase 4: rules generated from the register + drift test; close the exposure rows.
- Move remaining Admin SDK jobs onto `SYSTEM_JOBS`; App Check enforcement when metrics are healthy.

## Owner ask

Evaluate current data classification, propose an architecture, then (after approval) build classification logic + workflow, with a Master Admin **Security → Data classification** tab. Datapoints: `isPatronAccessible`, `isRegularEmployeeAccessible`, `isPrivilegedEmployeeAccessible`, `isClubAdminAccessible`, `isMasterAdminAccessible`. Translation required.

## Evaluation — where we are today

| Area | Today | Gap |
|---|---|---|
| Classification register | **None.** No collection/field carries a sensitivity label. | No single answer to “who may see field X”. |
| Firestore rules (~125 collections) | ~24 public-read (venues, events, templates, Display kiosk docs), ~32 Master-only (logs, audit, ledgers), the rest scoped per collection with hand-written helpers. | Rules are correct per collection but not derived from a policy; new collections get ad-hoc rules. |
| Over-exposure (open findings) | `users` (email, phone) readable by **any signed-in user**; `inboxNotifications` readable by any signed-in user; `clubEmployeeDesignations` open to signed-in users. | PII spillage across patrons / clubs. |
| Roles | Spread across `feature-gates.js`, `floqr-feature-services.js`, `shared-data.js` email lists, `functions/*` `PRIVILEGED_EMAILS`, rules `isMasterAdmin()` (claim **or** email list, lists disagree `bands.don` vs `bans.don`). | No single role source; no custom claims for club roles. |
| FloqAi / help | Audience tags `patron / serviceMember / venueAdmin / masterAdmin` filter help + intents **in the browser** (`canAccessHelpEntry`). | UX only — anything in the Pages bundle is readable by anyone. |
| Public feeds | `venuePublicFeed` (secret key, published data only, consent-gated staff photos). | Fine — becomes the model for “Public” class. |
| Logs | Twilio/mail/compliance logs Master-only, codes HMAC’d, audit chain for Features & Services. | Good; no field-level masking for Club Admin views of logs. |

## Proposed model

### 1. Five access tiers (the owner’s datapoints) + Public

Tiers are **cumulative upward** unless an item is explicitly “own record only”:

| Tier datapoint | Who | Resolved from |
|---|---|---|
| `isPatronAccessible` | any signed-in patron (own data + public data) | Firebase Auth |
| `isRegularEmployeeAccessible` | staff linked to the club (waiter, barman, bottle service, DJ…) | non-rejected `clubEmployeeDesignations` for that club |
| `isPrivilegedEmployeeAccessible` | staff with a designated permission (`manageSchedules`, door, cashier, manager) | `rolePermissions` on the designation |
| `isClubAdminAccessible` | Club Admin of that club | `clubAdminAssignments` active / club `adminUids` |
| `isMasterAdminAccessible` | Master Admin (all data) | custom claim `masterAdmin` (replaces email lists) |

Plus `public` (no sign-in: venue page, events, Display kiosk docs, feeds). Club tiers are **scoped to `locationId`** — a Club Admin of club A is a patron at club B.

Each item stores all five as `0|1` (owner’s naming), plus derived `classificationLevel`:

| Level | Meaning | Typical tier pattern (P / RE / PE / CA / MA) |
|---|---|---|
| `public` | Published on purpose | 1 1 1 1 1 + no sign-in |
| `internal` | Signed-in FLOQR users | 1 1 1 1 1 |
| `clubConfidential` | Club operations | 0 1 1 1 1 (schedules) or 0 0 1 1 1 (payroll-ish) |
| `restricted` | PII, payments, security logs | 0 0 0 1 1 (club-scoped) or 0 0 0 0 1 |
| `secret` | Credentials, OTP, hashes | never readable by clients (Functions only) |

Owner record (`own`) flag: patrons always read **their own** `users/{uid}`, orders, inbox — independent of tier.

### 2. Classification register (source of truth)

Firestore `dataClassification/{collection}` (+ optional `fields.{field}` overrides):

```
{ collection, description, owner: "club|patron|platform",
  scopeField: "locationId|clubLocationId|uid|null",
  isPatronAccessible, isRegularEmployeeAccessible, isPrivilegedEmployeeAccessible,
  isClubAdminAccessible, isMasterAdminAccessible, ownRecordReadable,
  classificationLevel, containsPII, retentionDays, fields: { email: {...same flags} },
  revision, updatedByEmail, updatedAtMs, lastChangeReason }
```

- Packaged default catalog in `functions/data-classification-core.js` (mirrored client `floqr-data-classification.js`, parity test — same pattern as Features & Services). Seeded on first Master Admin unlock.
- Writes only via SOS2FA callables (`setDataClassification`, reason ≥ 8, chained audit — reuse `featureServiceAuditLogs` chain or a sibling `dataSecurityAuditLogs`).

### 3. Enforcement (the important part — defense in depth)

1. **Rules generation, not runtime lookup.** Firestore rules cannot cheaply read a register per request. A build script `scripts/generate-classified-rules.js` turns the packaged catalog into rule helpers (`canReadClassified(collection, scopeId)`), and a test fails if `firestore.rules` drifts from the catalog. Changing a tier in the Master Admin tab marks “rules out of date” until the next rules deploy (shown as a red banner).
2. **Custom claims for roles** (`masterAdmin`, `clubRoles: {locationId: "RE|PE|CA"}`), written by a trigger on designations/assignments, so rules can check tiers without extra reads. Removes the email lists (fixes the `bands/bans` mismatch).
3. **Sensitive-field split** — PII leaves broadly-readable docs: `users/{uid}` keeps public profile fields; email/phone move to `userPrivate/{uid}` (self + Master Admin, Club Admin via callable with reason). Same for `clubEmployeeDesignations` contact fields. This closes open findings 1–3.
4. **Callable gate for cross-tier reads** — `getClassifiedRecords({collection, locationId, fields})` (Admin SDK) applies field masking from the register for Club Admin / staff views, logs `data.read` for `restricted`.
5. **FloqAi server answers** — `getFloqAiIntents` returns only intents/help whose classification fits the caller (moves the filter off the client).
6. **DLP checks** — Functions tests scan help bodies, public feeds, and Display docs for PII patterns (email, phone, tokens); outbound email/SMS templates validated against `restricted` fields; feed/iframe output already allow-lists fields.

### 4. Master Admin → Security → Data classification (tab)

- Sub-tab `dataClassification` in `#securitySubtabs` (`master.securityDataClassification`).
- Grid: collection · level badge · five tier toggles (P / RE / PE / CA / MA) · own-record · PII · retention · “rules in sync” dot.
- Row drawer: field overrides, description, owner, change history (from the audit chain).
- Actions: Save (SOS2FA + reason), Verify audit chain, Export register (CSV/JSON for auditors), **Access simulator** (“what can a Regular Employee of Club X read?”) and **Exposure report** (collections where rules are broader than the register — today: `users`, `inboxNotifications`, `clubEmployeeDesignations`).
- i18n: Master-only help may stay English per translation policy, but the tab chrome keys go into all 11 packs (chrome parity).

### 5. Rollout phases (each shippable)

1. Register + packaged catalog + Master Admin tab (read-only simulator + exposure report). No behavior change.
2. Custom claims trigger; rules accept claim **or** legacy check.
3. PII split (`userPrivate`, designation contacts) + rule tightening for the three open findings; migration script (dry-run first, owner go-ahead before running on production).
4. Rules generator + drift test; register edits become “pending deploy”.
5. Server-side FloqAi intents; DLP content tests; field masking callable.

### Risks

- Phase 3 touches every page that reads another user’s email/phone (Employee Network, picker, scheduling notify). Needs a full click-path pass.
- Rules size limits (~256 KB / 1000 expressions) — generator must emit shared helpers, not per-field expressions.
- Club staff with multiple clubs → claims map can grow; cap and fall back to callable.

